\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {First three principal-component loadings of monthly US Treasury curve changes, 2019 to current.} PC1 (level, blue) is roughly flat across maturities, indicating a parallel shift. PC2 (slope, red) is monotonically increasing with maturity, flipping sign around the 3- to 5-year maturity. PC3 (curvature, green) is humped with peaks at the short and long ends and a trough in the intermediate maturities. The three components together explain 99.9 per cent of variation.}}{6}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:pca}{{4}{6}{\textbf {First three principal-component loadings of monthly US Treasury curve changes, 2019 to current.} PC1 (level, blue) is roughly flat across maturities, indicating a parallel shift. PC2 (slope, red) is monotonically increasing with maturity, flipping sign around the 3- to 5-year maturity. PC3 (curvature, green) is humped with peaks at the short and long ends and a trough in the intermediate maturities. The three components together explain 99.9 per cent of variation}{figure.caption.6}{}}
